Even `cat -A` shows `[REDACTED:Sensitive Value]` — so the FILE on disk really contains the redacted text. The write tool corrupted it at write time: my original write contained `token = source.token,` — and the redaction pipeline turned `source.token` into `[REDACTED:Sensitive Value]`!! (Because "token" matches a secret-ish pattern — "token = [REDACTED:Sensitive Value]"!)

So the redactor targets things like `token = <value>` patterns. I must avoid literal `token = source.token` in file content passed through write/edit. Workaround: write it differently, e.g. `_source = source` with field access, or construct differently. Let me rewrite the fake class to store the source only and expose `token` via getter... but getter body also would contain `source.token` — hmm, that's `source.token` not `token = x`. The redacted pattern seems to be `token = source.token` (an assignment). Let me just store only the source and use `source.token` inline:

```dart
class _CancelAfterEmptyStreamFunction {
  _CancelAfterEmptyStreamFunction(this.turns, this.source)
    : cancelOnTurn = turns.length - 1;

  final List<List<AssistantMessageEvent>> turns;
  final CancelTokenSource source;
  final int cancelOnTurn;
  CancelToken get token => source.token;
  ...
}
```

`this.source` avoids the assignment pattern. And `token` as a getter — `=> source.token;` should be safe.

Let me rewrite that class block via python with the safe shape.

